# What happens when a user writes a failing test: `aipl check` reports each
# failed assertion (with its source location) under a per-test FAIL header and
# exits non-zero. The `--- check ---` section below pins that exact report.
# `one`'s test passes (so it's silent); only `answer`'s test fails.

import { equal as ==, greater_than as > } from builtins;

fn one() -> i64 { 1 }.test({
    assert(one() == 1);
})

fn answer() -> i64 { 41 }.test({
    assert(answer() == 42);
    assert(answer() > 100);
})

fn main() {}
--- check ---
./tests/cases/tests/failing_test.aipl:
    one: 1 assertion passed
    answer: 0 assertions passed, 2 failed
test failures:
test answer ... FAIL
  assert failed at ./tests/cases/tests/failing_test.aipl:13 answer() == 42
  assert failed at ./tests/cases/tests/failing_test.aipl:14 answer() > 100
2 tests: 1 passed, 1 failed
